---
// Generated by GSA-Per-Diem/tools/export_legal_to_website.mjs from the app's legal.json. Edit that file instead.
type Props = {
  contactEmail: string;
};

const { contactEmail } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="legal-content">
  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>1. Overview</h2>
    <p>
      This Privacy Policy explains how Restart Apps ("we", "us", or "our") handles information in US
      Per Diem (the "App") on Android and iOS. The App looks up federal per diem rates and builds
      trip reports. It does not have accounts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>2. Information you provide</h2>
    <p>
      The App does not ask for your name, email address, phone number, or location. The states,
      fiscal years, cities, and trip dates you choose are used on your device to look up rates and
      build reports. They are kept only while the App is open and are not sent to us.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you contact support by email, we receive your email address and whatever you include in
      your message, and we use them only to respond to you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>3. Information stored on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The App keeps a few settings on your device: your appearance choice (System, Light, or Dark),
      your usage-analytics and crash-report choices, simple counters used to decide when to show an
      app-open ad or ask for a store review, and, where required, the ad consent choices saved by
      Google's consent tool. This information stays on your device and is removed when you delete
      the App.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>4. Rate data requests</h2>
    <p>
      To look up rates, the App sends requests to the U.S. General Services Administration (GSA)
      and, for Alaska, Hawaii, and U.S. territories, the Defense Travel Management Office (DTMO). A
      request can include the state, city or locality, and fiscal year, and DTMO requests include
      the dates whose rates are being looked up. Your trip dates themselves are not sent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Like any website, these services may receive standard network information such as your IP
      address and device request details.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>5. Advertising, consent, and tracking</h2>
    <p>
      The App uses Google AdMob to show banner and app-open ads. Google and its partners may collect
      or receive device identifiers, advertising identifiers, IP address, app interaction data,
      diagnostic data, and information about ad views or ad interactions to provide, measure, and
      improve ads.
    </p>
    <p>
      The App uses Google User Messaging Platform (UMP) to request and store advertising consent
      where required, including in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, and Switzerland.
      When Google requires it, an Ad privacy choices option appears in Settings so you can review or
      change those choices.
    </p>
    <p>
      On iOS, the App may ask for permission through Apple App Tracking Transparency before allowing
      advertising tracking; you can change this later in iOS Settings. On Android, the App declares
      the Google advertising ID permission used by Google advertising services, and you can reset or
      delete your advertising ID in Android settings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>6. Analytics and crash reports</h2>
    <p>
      The App uses Firebase Analytics and Firebase Crashlytics to understand how the App is used,
      diagnose crashes, and improve reliability. Analytics events record actions such as opening the
      App, moving between screens, completing a rate lookup or trip report, and exporting a file.
      They may include general categories, such as whether rates came from GSA or DTMO, an
      approximate trip length range, and whether an action succeeded. They do not include the
      states, cities, or dates you choose, report amounts, or the contents of exported files.
    </p>
    <p>
      Crash reports include technical details about the error and the screen in use. Firebase may
      also collect device information and identifiers as described in Google's privacy
      documentation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Usage analytics and crash reports are separate choices. Where the law requires consent first,
      such as in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, and Switzerland, usage analytics
      stays off until you agree in the consent message; elsewhere both are on until you change them.
      You can turn either off at any time in Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Legal; the change applies
      immediately and is remembered on your device. Turning crash reports off also discards any
      reports that have not been sent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>7. Store reviews</h2>
    <p>
      After you have used the App for a while, it may ask for a rating using the in-app review
      feature of Google Play, the Apple App Store, or the Samsung Galaxy Store. Any review you leave
      is handled by that store under its own policies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>8. Exports and local files</h2>
    <p>
      When you export a PDF or CSV report, the file is created on your device. Android 10 and later
      save exports to the Downloads folder; Android 9 and earlier save them to the App's own folder,
      which needs no storage permission. iOS creates a temporary file and opens the system Open In
      menu or share sheet. The App does not upload exported reports; sharing them is controlled by
      you and the apps or services you choose.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>9. Permissions</h2>
    <p>
      Internet: used to fetch per diem rates and load ads.
    </p>
    <p>
      Advertising identifier: used by Google advertising services where available and permitted.
    </p>
    <p>
      The App does not request access to your location, contacts, camera, photos, or storage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>10. Third-party services</h2>
    <p>
      The App uses Google AdMob, Google User Messaging Platform, Google Play services on Android,
      Firebase Analytics and Crashlytics, store review features from Google Play, Apple, and
      Samsung, GSA APIs, and DTMO rate pages. Their privacy practices are governed by their own
      policies.
    </p>
    <p>
      See <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's Privacy Policy</a>, <a
      href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase privacy information</a>, and <a
      href="https://www.gsa.gov/website-information/website-policies">GSA's website policies</a>.
      Official rates: <a href="https://www.gsa.gov/travel/plan-a-trip/per-diem-rates">GSA per diem
      rates</a> and <a
      href="https://www.travel.dod.mil/Travel-Transportation-Rates/Per-Diem/Per-Diem-Rate-Lookup/">DTMO
      non-foreign OCONUS rates</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>11. Your choices and data deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Because the App has no accounts, we do not keep a profile about you. You can turn off usage
      analytics and crash reports in Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Legal, change ad privacy choices
      where offered, and limit ad tracking in your device settings. Deleting the App removes
      everything it stored on your device.
    </p>
    <p>
      To ask about data held by Firebase on our behalf, email <a
      href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>12. Children's privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The App is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect personal
      information from children under 13.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>13. Security</h2>
    <p>
      Rate requests and Firebase and ad traffic use encrypted connections. No method of electronic
      transmission or storage is completely secure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>14. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. Changes take effect when they are posted
      in the App or on the App listing, and the effective date above will change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="legal-block" data-reveal>
    <h2>15. Contact us</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this Privacy Policy can be sent to <a
      href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
